Results and discussion
General growth patterns
General growth patterns of BSF larvae were examined using experimental data from literature. Fig 2A and 2B show WW and DW of BSF larvae and prepupae as function of the time it took for them to reach either the prepupal stage, tp or their maximal weight. The maximal weight is reached shortly before the individuals can be identified as prepupae [9] and these larvae are here denoted full-grown. There is a tendency for the BSF larvae and prepupae to grow heavier the shorter their development time is. Fig 2C and 2D show that the larvae also tend to obtain higher lipid contents the heavier they become (r2 = 0.42), and their DW content is positively related to their lipid content. In contrast seems there to be no apparent relationships between protein content and DW (r2 = 0.09), protein content and lipid content (r2 = 0.04), nor ash content and DW (r2 = 0.07), see S1 Fig. It was due to these findings that storage lipids were
